  <div class="quick-answer">
    <p><strong>Quick facts:</strong> Gilimanuk Ferry Port runs 24/7. Ferries to Ketapang (Java) depart every 15–30 minutes. Crossing time: 30–45 minutes. Adult foot passenger fare: from IDR 10,600. Book tickets online via Ferizy. Drive time to Ubud: 3.5–4.5 hours. Port closes on Nyepi only.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Gilimanuk is the only official ferry crossing between Bali and Java. If you're arriving from East Java — or heading there — this port is your entry and exit point. This guide covers everything: ferry hours, ticket prices, how to book online, what to expect at the port, and how to get from Gilimanuk to wherever you're staying in Bali.</p>

  <h2>Where Is Gilimanuk?</h2>

  <p>Gilimanuk sits on the far western tip of Bali, in Jembrana Regency. The town faces Ketapang Port in Banyuwangi, East Java, across the Bali Strait — the narrowest point between the two islands, roughly 3 km of open water.</p>

  <p>The port is 130 km west of Ubud and about 134 km from Denpasar. If you're coming from southern Bali, expect a long drive before you even get on the boat.</p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>Important:</strong> The ferry does not depart from Banyuwangi city. It leaves from Ketapang Port, approximately 10 km north of Banyuwangi town. Don't book a ride to the wrong terminal.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Gilimanuk Ferry Schedule and Operating Hours</h2>

  <p>Gilimanuk Ferry Port is open 24 hours a day, every day of the year — with one major exception.</p>

  <p>Ferries depart approximately every 15–30 minutes throughout the day. At night, frequency drops to roughly every 30 minutes, but crossings continue through the early hours.</p>

  <p>During Indonesian national holidays — particularly Idul Fitri, Christmas, and New Year (locally called Nataru) — passenger and vehicle volumes surge significantly. Queues can extend for several hours at these times. If your trip overlaps with any public holiday, arrive early or book a private transfer that picks you up at a set time.</p>

  <div class="info-box">
    <h3>Nyepi Warning</h3>
    <p>On Bali's Day of Silence, Gilimanuk Ferry Port closes completely for 24 hours. No crossings operate. Plan your Java-Bali route at least one full day before or after Nyepi. Check the Balinese Hindu calendar for the exact date each year — it shifts annually.</p>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Crossing duration:</strong> The ferry takes approximately 30–45 minutes on the water. Add loading and unloading time, and allow a total of 45–90 minutes from boarding to walking off the other side. During peak periods, you may wait offshore for an available berth, which can add another hour.</p>

  <h2>Gilimanuk Ferry Ticket Prices (2026)</h2>

  <p>Fares are set by the government operator PT ASDP Indonesia Ferry (Persero) and apply equally in both directions.</p>

