  <h2>How to Book Ferry Tickets: Ferizy</h2>

  <p>Gilimanuk is one of Indonesia's four major ferry ports (alongside Merak, Bakauheni, and Ketapang) where you can no longer buy tickets inside the port itself. You must buy your ticket in advance via the official Ferizy platform.</p>

  <p><strong>How to book:</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Download the <strong>Ferizy app</strong> from the Play Store (Android) or App Store (iOS), or visit ferizy.com</li>
    <li>Register an account using your email, Google account, or Facebook</li>
    <li>Select your origin port (Gilimanuk or Ketapang), destination, vehicle type, departure schedule, and number of passengers</li>
    <li>If travelling with a vehicle, enter your vehicle registration number exactly as shown on your STNK (vehicle registration document)</li>
    <li>Complete payment — your e-ticket is sent to your email and stored in the app</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Bring your e-ticket to the port. The system uses geofencing, so your ticket becomes active only when you're within a defined radius of the port.</p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>For tourists without an Indonesian ID (NIK):</strong> The Ferizy system primarily targets Indonesian residents. International travellers can still buy foot passenger tickets at roadside ticket booths near the port approach road. Watch for touts along this road — some inflate prices. If in doubt, use the Ferizy app or buy at the official ASDP counter inside the port perimeter.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What to Expect at the Port</h2>

  <p>Gilimanuk is a working port, not a tourist terminal. Expect a busy, functional environment with limited English signage.</p>

  <p><strong>Security checks:</strong> Since 2002, security at Gilimanuk has been thorough. All passengers go through a security check. For Indonesian nationals travelling from Java to Bali, security officers check ID and may ask the purpose of your visit. Prepare for queues — particularly during peak hours. Carry your passport (for foreigners) or KTP (for Indonesian nationals) at all times.</p>

  <p><strong>Vehicle lanes:</strong> Cars drive through their own lane. Motorbikes use a dedicated scooter lane off to the side of the main vehicle flow — easy to miss if you've never crossed before. Follow port staff directions and look for signs at the lane split.</p>

  <p><strong>On board the ferry:</strong> Once on the ferry, secure your vehicle, take your valuables, and head upstairs to the passenger deck. Most ferries have a small canteen selling coffee, snacks, instant noodles, and cold drinks. The views of the Bali Strait are worth stepping outside for.</p>

  <p><strong>Facilities at the port:</strong> Basic waiting areas, public toilets, food stalls, and ATMs are available on both sides of the crossing. Don't rely on finding currency exchange; bring Indonesian Rupiah before you arrive.</p>

