  <p>Traffic on the Denpasar–Gilimanuk highway is generally light compared to southern Bali. The route is scenic, passing rice fields, coastal towns, and sections of the West Bali National Park.</p>

  <h3>By public bus (Elf minibus)</h3>

  <p>Public minibuses (locally called Elf) run from Ubung bus terminal in Denpasar to Gilimanuk. The journey takes 3–4 hours and costs around IDR 60,000–70,000 per person. Schedules are irregular and buses don't run at night. This is viable for budget travellers, not recommended if you're catching a specific ferry time or have significant luggage.</p>

  <p>From Ubud: Take Trans Metro Dewata (TMD) bus Corridor 4 to Ngurah Rai Stadium, switch to Corridor 2 to Ubung terminal, then board an Elf to Gilimanuk. Total cost approximately IDR 64,400 (TMD: IDR 4,400 + Elf: IDR 60,000).</p>

  <p>From Kuta or the airport: Take TMD Corridor 1 or 2 to Ubung terminal, then an Elf to Gilimanuk.</p>

  <p>Grab and Gojek drivers rarely go as far as Gilimanuk, and fares for ride-hailing apps can be unpredictable on this route.</p>

  <h2>Getting from Gilimanuk Port to Your Bali Destination</h2>

  <p>Most travellers arriving from Java want to get directly to Ubud, Canggu, Seminyak, Kuta, or another southern Bali destination. Here's how.</p>

  <h3>Private transfer (recommended)</h3>

  <p>A pre-booked private car with driver is the most reliable option. You step off the ferry and your driver meets you at the port exit. No negotiating, no waiting for a shared shuttle, no language barrier.</p>

