  <h2>What's Near Gilimanuk Port?</h2>

  <p>If you have time before or after your ferry, these spots are within easy reach:</p>

  <p><strong>Gilimanuk Archaeological Museum:</strong> A small museum holding the earliest evidence of human habitation in Bali — ancient skeletal remains and artefacts dating back thousands of years. Entry is inexpensive and the museum is directly in Gilimanuk town. Useful for a 30–60 minute stop while waiting for a departure.</p>

  <p><strong>West Bali National Park:</strong> The national park begins just east of Gilimanuk. Mangrove forests, diverse birdlife, and wild Javan deer are among the draws. If you're travelling by private car, ask your driver to pass through the park section of the highway — the scenery is noticeably different from the rest of Bali.</p>

  <p><strong>Menjangan Island:</strong> One of Bali's best dive sites, accessible by boat from Labuan Lalang within the national park. If you're in no rush, this makes an excellent overnight stop before continuing to southern Bali.</p>

  <p><strong>Pemuteran:</strong> A quiet beach village about 45 minutes east of Gilimanuk, known for calm seas and coral restoration projects. A solid option if you want to break the journey south.</p>

  <p><strong>Ayam Betutu:</strong> Gilimanuk is locally famous for this dish — smoked, slow-cooked spiced chicken that's a specialty of West Bali. Try it at one of the roadside warungs near the bus terminal before or after your crossing.</p>

  <h2>Tips for a Smooth Crossing</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Book your Ferizy ticket early</strong> during school holidays, Idul Fitri, and Christmas/New Year. Ticket availability can run out for specific time slots.</li>
    <li><strong>Try for a daytime crossing.</strong> Views are better and the port is easier to navigate in daylight. At night, boarding and vehicle lanes are harder to read.</li>
    <li><strong>Arrive early.</strong> Even with a Ferizy ticket, port congestion during peak periods can mean queuing to enter the vehicle lanes. Arrive at least 45 minutes before your chosen departure time if you're bringing a vehicle.</li>
    <li><strong>Take your valuables off the vehicle</strong> before boarding. Leave nothing visible in your car or on your motorbike — ferry decks are open and your vehicle is unattended while you're on the passenger deck.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the Nyepi date</strong> before booking any Java-Bali crossing in February or March. The date shifts each year. Getting stranded on either side of the strait for 24 hours is avoidable if you check early.</li>
    <li><strong>Have cash in IDR.</strong> Port food stalls, warung, and some ticket sellers don't accept cards or digital payment. Carry small denominations.</li>
  </ul>

